The attached image illustrates Nano-11's ability to enhance antigen targeting to draining lymph node cells and improve antigen retention at the injection site, a crucial aspect of vaccine adjuvancy. Part A shows that migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) express the highest levels of CD86, a costimulatory molecule essential for T cell activation. Part B further characterizes OVA-positive cells in the draining lymph node, indicating that MigDCs, particularly CD11b+ DCs and MoDCs, are the primary cells internalizing the adjuvanted antigen. Part C, using in vivo imaging, demonstrates that Nano-11, similar to aluminum hydroxide, significantly prolongs the presence of fluorescently labeled OVA at the injection site over several days compared to soluble OVA, which rapidly clears.